(begin-tx "STAGEZ-02 — the chain-agnostic readers, and the one that is not")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 10000000)(env-gas 0)
;;==== STAGEZ-02 — the chain-agnostic readers, and the one that is not · 01 · namespace ==== ======
(print "--- [STAGEZ-02 — the chain-agnostic readers, and the one that is not · 01 · namespace] ---")
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
(print "--- [STAGEZ-02 · 01 · readers that work on any chain] ---")
;;URC_PrimordialIDs derives every id from DALOS rather than hardcoding, so it works on any chain.
;;It is asserted against the sandbox's own hash to show the derivation really is dynamic — on a
;;chain with a different deployment hash this same call returns that hash instead.
(map print
    [
        (expect "<<STAGEZ-02>> the primordial ids are DERIVED, so they carry this chain's hash"
            ["OURO-98c486052a51" "GAS-98c486052a51" "AURYN-98c486052a51"
             "ELITEAURYN-98c486052a51" "DWK-98c486052a51" "DLK-98c486052a51"]
            [(DALOS.UR_OuroborosID) (DALOS.UR_IgnisID) (DALOS.UR_AurynID)
             (DALOS.UR_EliteAurynID) (DALOS.UR_WrappedStoaID) (DALOS.UR_SilverStoaID)])
        ;;URC_TrueFungibleAmountPrice's linearity was pinned here. Deleted with the function:
        ;;zero callers anywhere, and no AppReads successor, because no page multiplies an
        ;;amount by a price on chain -- they read both and multiply in the client.
        ;;EXPLORER's per-account reader is chain-agnostic -- and must AGREE with the sovereign
        ;;source it wraps. Asserted against DALOS rather than against a literal, so it keeps
        ;;holding as the account transacts; what is pinned is that the explorer does not drift
        ;;from the ledger it reports on.
        (expect "<<STAGEZ-02>> the explorer's account nonce agrees with DALOS's"
            (DALOS.UR_AccountNonce KST.ANHD) (EXPLORER.UR_0001_AccountNonce KST.ANHD))
        ;;--- AND THE ONE THAT WAS A FINDING. This used to be an `expect-failure` pinning the
        ;;landing page as mainnet-only: it hardcoded the Auryndex pair id, so it aborted with
        ;;"No value found in table" on any sandbox. The comment beside it said "if that is ever
        ;;made chain-agnostic this assertion will start failing -- which is the intent. Change it
        ;;to a success assertion at that point." That point arrived on 2026-09-24, when the same
        ;;defect in DPL-UR took the UI dashboard down and both modules were switched to derived
        ;;ids. So: flipped, as instructed.
        ;;
        ;;The PROOF lives in STAGEZ-04, not here, and the reason is instructive: clearing the id
        ;;blocker did not make this function callable from a plain assertion, because the OTHER
        ;;blocker -- the admin-gated cross-module scan -- is untouched and is not a defect. So the
        ;;positive assertion needs `env-module-admin` to model the `/local` context a UI reader is
        ;;actually invoked in, and it belongs beside the block that explains that distinction.
        (expect-failure "<<STAGEZ-02>> the landing page no longer dies on a MAINNET ID -- what \
            \ refuses it now is the local-only scan, proved and then cleared in STAGEZ-04"
            "Module admin necessary for operation but has not been acquired:ouronet-ns.DALOS"
            (EXPLORER.URC_0001_LandingPage))
    ]
)
(commit-tx)
;;NOT COVERED: URC_PrimordialPrices needs live SWP pools (this chain is deploy-only, no [6.3]_SWP);
;;it is reachable from a suite that builds pools first.
;;
;;CORRECTION: this note used to claim URC_0035_EliteAccountRichList "is a heavy account scan that
;;aborts here", i.e. a missing-fixture problem. That was wrong: it aborts on a module-admin gate
;;BEFORE scanning anything, so building the fixture would not have helped and would have looked
;;like the fix failing for some other reason. It is pinned in STAGEZ-06d.
;;
;;SECOND CORRECTION (2026-09-12): I then over-corrected, and filed that abort as a MAINNET DEFECT --
;;"six UI readers uncallable on every chain". Owner ruling: "keys can be called freely, there is no
;;admin gating on this." The abort is real but it is TRANSACTIONAL-MODE-ONLY, and a REPL only has
;;transactional mode. Nodes run with `--allowReadsInLocal` (verified on this project's own nodes),
;;so `/local` reads -- which is how a UI reader is invoked -- are unrestricted. STAGEZ-04/05/06 now
;;read as what they actually measure: these readers are LOCAL-ONLY.
;;|| NEXT >
;;
(begin-tx "STAGEZ-03 -- the TESTING VARIANT, and which blocker is which")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 10000000)(env-gas 0)
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
;;==== STAGEZ-03 -- the TESTING VARIANT, and which blocker is which · 01 · env-sigs (caps) ==== ===
(print "--- [STAGEZ-03 -- the TESTING VARIANT, and which blocker is which · 01 · env-sigs (caps)] ---")
(env-sigs
  [{"key": "1a4e15d3c51e0b73e92644600487ba8eaae312e1a178b91801d54e13c1b350a5", "caps": []}])
;;==== STAGEZ-03 . 01 . the testing variant is RETIRED ====
(print "--- [STAGEZ-03 . 01 . the testing variant is RETIRED] ---")
;;This block used to deploy _generated/02_EXPLORER-TESTING.pact, a generated near-copy of canonical
;;EXPLORER carrying sandbox ATS pair ids in place of the mainnet ones. It existed because canonical
;;hardcoded those ids and therefore could not run here at all.
;;
;;Canonical now DERIVES them (2026-09-24), so it runs in this fixture directly and the variant had
;;nothing left to substitute. Testing the real module instead of a near-copy is strictly better:
;;coverage tooling used to report canonical URC_0001_LandingPage as unreached and be CORRECT about
;;it, because the thing under test was not the thing that ships.
;;
;;Worth knowing how the variant ended: its substitution list carried an exact occurrence count
;;specifically to refuse a no-op, but counted over the raw file INCLUDING COMMENTS. When the fix
;;named the old ids in an explanatory comment, the substitution matched the comment, met its count,
;;and generated a variant identical to canonical -- silently, with the gate green.
(print "--- [STAGEZ-03 . 01 . (no variant to deploy; canonical is chain-agnostic)] ---")
(commit-tx)

;;|| NEXT >
;;
(begin-tx "STAGEZ-04 -- URC_0001_LandingPage has TWO blockers, not one")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 10000000)(env-gas 0)
;;==== STAGEZ-04 -- URC_0001_LandingPage has TWO blockers, not one · 01 · namespace ==== ==========
(print "--- [STAGEZ-04 -- URC_0001_LandingPage has TWO blockers, not one · 01 · namespace] ---")
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
(print "--- [STAGEZ-04 . 01 . isolating blocker 1 from blocker 2] ---")
;;This pair of assertions is the whole point of the variant. It SEPARATES two independent reasons
;;this function cannot run in a REPL, by construction rather than by argument:
;;
;;  blocker 1 -- hardcoded mainnet ids, and a REAL portability defect. Canonical binds
;;               (ATS::URC_Index "Auryndex-O136CBn22ncY") in its `let`. Pact `let` is EAGER, so
;;               canonical dies there and never reaches its own body -- on any chain that is not
;;               mainnet. Substituting the sandbox ids removes this; that is all the variant does.
;;
;;  blocker 2 -- NOT a defect. Visible only once blocker 1 is gone. The body calls
;;               (keys DALOS.DALOS|AccountTable): a scan of ANOTHER module's table, which Pact
;;               admin-gates in TRANSACTIONAL mode. A REPL is always transactional, so it aborts
;;               here. Chainweb nodes run `--allowReadsInLocal`, so the same call in a `/local`
;;               query -- the only way a UI reader is ever invoked -- is unrestricted. The REPL
;;               cannot model this: `FlagAllowReadInLocal` is a node exec flag, not a repl flag.
;;
;;So the variant does not "make the landing page work" in a REPL; it clears the portability blocker
;;and leaves the local-only property exposed, which is what the second assertion pins. The honest
;;reading of that assertion is "this function is LOCAL-ONLY", not "this function is broken".
;;If it is ever routed through an in-module reader (STAGEZ-05's legal form) it becomes callable
;;inside a transaction and the assertion flips to a success case.
;;
;;I had this filed as "six UI readers dead on mainnet" for several passes. It was wrong, and the
;;lesson is worth more than the finding was: an error reproduced in a REPL is an error in a REPL.
;;Before calling it a production defect, establish that production runs in the same execution mode
;;the test used. Here it does not. Checked before withdrawing: all six readers have ZERO callers in
;;Pact code, so nothing transactional reaches them either.
(map print
    [
        ;;BLOCKER 1 IS GONE -- canonical derives its ids now, so it no longer dies in its eager
        ;;`let`, and the pair of assertions that separated the two blockers has collapsed to one.
        ;;What survives is the half that was never a defect: the cross-module scan. Canonical now
        ;;demonstrates it directly, which is what the variant was standing in for.
        (expect-failure "<<STAGEZ-04>> canonical is LOCAL-ONLY: the cross-module scan is admin-gated"
            "Module admin necessary for operation but has not been acquired:ouronet-ns.DALOS"
            (EXPLORER.URC_0001_LandingPage))
    ]
)
;;NOW GRANT THE ADMIN AND RUN IT. This models the `/local` context with --allowReadsInLocal, which
;;is the only way a UI reader is ever invoked -- see the blocker-2 note above. Order matters: the
;;`expect-failure` above must run BEFORE this line, or the very gate it pins is already lifted.
(env-module-admin ouronet-ns.DALOS)
;;The assertion is on the index NAMES, not the ids. A name is stable across chains; an id is not,
;;so pinning an id here would rebuild the exact coupling that caused the 2026-09-24 outage. That
;;these come back at all is the proof: before the fix this function could not reach its own body
;;on any chain but mainnet.
(map print
    [
        (expect "<<STAGEZ-04>> ...and once local, it DERIVES the Auryndex pair on this chain"
            "Auryndex"
            (at "z2-t1" (EXPLORER.URC_0001_LandingPage)))
        (expect "<<STAGEZ-04>> ...and the EliteAuryndex pair"
            "EliteAuryndex"
            (at "z2-t2" (EXPLORER.URC_0001_LandingPage)))
    ]
)
(commit-tx)

;;|| NEXT >
;;
(begin-tx "STAGEZ-05 -- blocker 2 is a real defect: the legal form of the same read works")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 10000000)(env-gas 0)
;;==== STAGEZ-05 -- blocker 2 is a real defect: the legal form of the same read works · 01 · namespace ==== 
(print "--- [STAGEZ-05 -- blocker 2 is a real defect: the legal form of the same read works · 01 · namespace] ---")
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
(print "--- [STAGEZ-05 . 01 . cross-module scan vs in-module scan] ---")
;;The two forms, side by side, so the rule is pinned independently of EXPLORER: a cross-module scan
;;is admin-gated in transactional mode, while the identical read performed INSIDE the module that
;;owns the table is legal and returns a value. DALOS already ships that reader.
;;
;;This is the shape to use IF a transactional caller is ever needed -- route through the owning
;;module's ref. It is NOT a defect to be fixed everywhere: for a `/local` UI reader the direct scan
;;is fine, because nodes run `--allowReadsInLocal`. `_conformance.py --rule cross-module-scan`
;;keeps the list (10 sites) as an OBSERVATION / watch list: the day one of those functions is called
;;from a `C_` or `A_` path, it becomes a real defect at that moment, and the rule is how that gets
;;noticed. See memories/2026-09-11-stage-z-cross-module-keys.md.
(map print
    [
        ;;the ILLEGAL form, asserted directly so the rule is pinned independently of EXPLORER
        (expect-failure "<<STAGEZ-05>> scanning another module's table is admin-gated"
            "Module admin necessary for operation but has not been acquired:ouronet-ns.DALOS"
            (length (keys DALOS.DALOS|AccountTable)))
        ;;the LEGAL form -- same data, read from inside the owning module
        (expect "<<STAGEZ-05>> the in-module reader DALOS ships for it works"
            true (contains "real Accounts!" (DALOS.URH_AccountCounter)))
    ]
)
(commit-tx)

;;|| NEXT >
;;
(begin-tx "STAGEZ-06 -- the same defect in DPL-UR, with no id problem anywhere near it")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 10000000)(env-gas 0)
;;==== STAGEZ-06 -- the same defect in DPL-UR, with no id problem anywhere near it · 01 · namespace ==== 
(print "--- [STAGEZ-06 -- the same defect in DPL-UR, with no id problem anywhere near it · 01 · namespace] ---")
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
(print "--- [STAGEZ-06 . 01 . four more UI entry points, dead for the same reason] ---")
;;STAGEZ-04/05 could be read as "an EXPLORER quirk tangled up with the mainnet ids". These four are
;;the control. NONE of them hardcodes an id -- they take an account and read the current chain --
;;and every one shows the same local-only property. Note the module named in each message differs
;;(DPTF / DPOF / DPDC / DALOS): that is the table each one reaches into, which is what makes this a
;;PATTERN in the codebase rather than a single slip.
;;
;;These four plus STAGEZ-04's landing page are the five (with URC_0035 below, six) Stage-Z readers
;;that cannot execute inside a transaction. That is a fact about them, NOT a defect: they are UI
;;readers served over `/local`, where `--allowReadsInLocal` applies, and none of them has a single
;;caller in Pact code. What these assertions buy is the boundary: if any of them ever acquires a
;;transactional caller, these tests are the record of what will happen.
(map print
    [
        (expect-failure "<<STAGEZ-06>> URC_0016_TruefungibleHeader scans DPTF's table"
            "Module admin necessary for operation but has not been acquired:ouronet-ns.DPTF"
            (O-UI-EIGHT.URH_01|Header KST.ANHD))
    ]
)
(commit-tx)

