(begin-tx "STAGEZ-20 -- drain a pool to empty (LAST block: this is destructive)")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 10000000)(env-gas 0)
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
;;==== STAGEZ-20 -- drain a pool to empty (LAST block: this is destructive) · 01 · env-sigs (caps) ==== 
(print "--- [STAGEZ-20 -- drain a pool to empty (LAST block: this is destructive) · 01 · env-sigs (caps)] ---")
(env-sigs [{ "key": "PK_AOZ", "caps": [] }])
(print "--- [STAGEZ-20 . 01 . full liquidity removal] ---")
;;SWPLC::UEV_AddLiquidity's "Liquidity Addition on an empty Pool must have a Balanced Part present!"
;;was UNPINNED (`python3 REPL/tools/_cheapseam.py`). It needs a pool with ZERO LP supply, and measurement
;;showed none exists: all 21 pools on this chain carry liquidity, and the issuance defpact creates
;;them WITH it, so there is no gap between creation and first deposit.
;;
;;A pool can however be drained. UEV_RemoveLiquidity permits `lp-amount <= pool-lp-amount` with no
;;retained minimum -- deliberately, per its own @doc: gating removal on the owner's add-switch
;;"isn't a safety mechanism, it's a trust violation". So full removal is a supported operation and
;;the empty state is reachable.
;;
;;The LP holder is KST.AOZT, not the usual patron: the issuance-only suite issues this pool under
;;AOZT, and its LP went there. Worth stating because the obvious guesses (ANHD, the SWP smart
;;account) all hold zero and make the drain look impossible.
;;
;;THIS BLOCK IS DESTRUCTIVE and is deliberately LAST in the file -- it empties a pool the earlier
;;blocks read.
(let* ((sp:string (at 0 (SWP.URC_ActiveSwpairs)))
       (lp:string (SWP.UR_TokenLP sp))
       (held:decimal (DPTF.UR_AccountSupply lp KST.AOZT)))
    (TS01-C3.SWP|C_RemoveLiquidity KST.AOZT KST.AOZT sp held)
    (map print
        [
            (expect "<<STAGEZ-20>> the holder really held the whole LP supply"
                true (> held 0.0))
            (expect "<<STAGEZ-20>> a full removal drains the pool to zero capacity"
                0.0 (SWP.URC_LpCapacity sp))
        ]
    )
)
(commit-tx)

;;|| NEXT >
;;
(begin-tx "STAGEZ-20b -- an empty pool refuses an unbalanced first deposit")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 10000000)(env-gas 0)
;;==== STAGEZ-20b -- an empty pool refuses an unbalanced first deposit · 01 · namespace ==== ======
(print "--- [STAGEZ-20b -- an empty pool refuses an unbalanced first deposit · 01 · namespace] ---")
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
(print "--- [STAGEZ-20b . 01 . the empty-pool balance rule] ---")
;;With no LP outstanding there is no ratio to price an unbalanced deposit against -- the first
;;deposit back into an empty pool defines the ratio, so it must carry a balanced part. Both sides
;;are asserted: an UNbalanced deposit is refused and a balanced one clears this guard.
;;==== STAGEZ-20b -- an empty pool refuses an unbalanced first deposit · 02 · let / invocation ==== 
(print "--- [STAGEZ-20b -- an empty pool refuses an unbalanced first deposit · 02 · let / invocation] ---")
(let ((sp:string (at 0 (SWP.URC_ActiveSwpairs))))
    (map print
        [
            (expect-failure "<<STAGEZ-20>> an UNBALANCED add to an empty pool is refused"
                "Liquidity Addition on an empty Pool must have a Balanced Part present!"
                (SWPLC.UEV_AddLiquidity sp
                    (SWPL.UDC_LiquidityData
                        (SWPL.UDC_LiquiditySplit [1.0 1.0] [0.0 0.0])
                        (SWPL.UDC_LiquiditySplitType false false) 0.0 0.0 0.0)))
            ;;...and a BALANCED one gets past it. Without this the assertion above would pass just
            ;;as well against a pool that refuses every deposit once empty.
            ;;
            ;;ARGUMENT ORDER TRAP: UDC_LiquiditySplitType is (iz-balanced, iz-asymmetric). Printing
            ;;the object shows "iz-asymmetric" FIRST because Pact renders object keys
            ;;ALPHABETICALLY, not in constructor order -- so reading the printed shape to infer the
            ;;argument order gets it backwards. (true false) is balanced-and-symmetric; (false true)
            ;;is the opposite and fires this very guard, which is how the mistake was caught.
            (expect "<<STAGEZ-20>> ...while a BALANCED add clears the same guard"
                true (!= 0 (length (format "{}"
                    [(SWPLC.UEV_AddLiquidity sp
                        (SWPL.UDC_LiquidityData
                            (SWPL.UDC_LiquiditySplit [1.0 1.0] [0.0 0.0])
                            (SWPL.UDC_LiquiditySplitType true false) 0.0 0.0 0.0))]))))
        ]
    )
)
(commit-tx)

(print "=== END modules/STAGE-Z.repl ===")

;;|| NEXT >
;;
(begin-tx "STAGEZ-21 -- the dispenser's stoicism-minting parity check")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 150000000)(env-gas 0)
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
;;==== STAGEZ-21 -- the dispenser's stoicism-minting parity check · 01 · env-sigs (caps) ==== =====
(print "--- [STAGEZ-21 -- the dispenser's stoicism-minting parity check · 01 · env-sigs (caps)] ---")
(env-sigs [{ "key": "PK_AncientHodler", "caps": [] }])
(print "--- [STAGEZ-21 . 01 . amounts and targets must agree in length] ---")
;;`DSP|STOICISM-MINTER` takes two parallel lists -- amounts and the accounts to mint them to -- and
;;refuses when they disagree. Argument-domain; the only reason it was unpinned is that nothing had called
;;the minter with a mismatched pair.
;;
;;DSP is a CITIZEN automaton with no harness of its own, and this file is the cheapest one that deploys
;;it. Both directions are driven, because the check is parity and a one-sided test would pass for a
;;guard written as a floor on either list.
(map print
    [
        (expect-failure "<<STAGEZ-21>> two amounts against one target is refused"
            "Length of stoicism-amounts and stoicism-targets must be the same"
            (DSP.A_StoicismMinter [1.0 2.0] [KST.EMMA]))
        (expect-failure "<<STAGEZ-21>> ...and so is one amount against two targets"
            "Length of stoicism-amounts and stoicism-targets must be the same"
            (DSP.A_StoicismMinter [1.0] [KST.EMMA KST.LUMY]))
    ]
)
(env-sigs [])
(commit-tx)
